        % New keys recognised
        issue
        articleno
        eid
        day
        doi
        url
        bookpages
        numpages
        lastaccessed
        coden
        isbn
        isbn-13
        issn
        lccn
  }
  {}
  { label.year extra.label sort.year sort.label basic.label.year}
```

*The first block (`ENTRY { … }`) tells the bibliography processor which fields may appear in an entry.  Anything that is **not** listed here will be silently ignored.*

The three empty braces `{}` after the field list are the *“entry type”* and *“entry macro”* sections – they are empty because you are not defining any special entry‑type‑specific behaviour yet.

The third brace group (`{ label.year … }`) is the *“sorting”* clause – it tells `bibtex` how to sort the bibliography.  You can leave it as‑is for now.

---

## 2.  Adding the new fields to the **output** part of the style

The snippet you posted also defines a few tiny helper functions:

```bibtex
FUNCTION { not }
{
    { #0 }
    { #1 }
  if$
}
FUNCTION { and }
{
    ’skip$
    { pop$ #0 }
  if$
}
FUNCTION { or }
{
   { pop$ #1 }
    ’skip$
  if$
}
FUNCTION { dump.stack.1 }
{
    duplicate$
```

These are the usual logical operators used by many `.bst` files.  You **do not** need to change them unless you want a different semantics for `and`, `or`, `not`.  (The `dump.stack.1` routine is a debugging aid – it prints the top of the stack.)

What you *do* need are **formatting functions** that turn the raw field values into the punctuation you want (e.g. “doi: 10.1000/xyz”, “URL <https://…>”, “ISBN 978‑3‑16‑148410‑0”, etc.) and **output routines** that decide *when* each field is printed.

Below is a **minimal but complete** example that:

1. Declares the extra fields (already done in the `ENTRY` block).
2. Provides a formatting routine for each of them.
3. Inserts the fields into the bibliography entry in a logical order.
4. Keeps the original standard fields (author, title, journal, …) untouched.

You can copy‑paste the whole file into a new file called `utah.bst` and then use it with `\bibliographystyle{utah}`.

---

### 2.1  Full “UTAH” `.bst` file (annotated)

```bibtex
% --------------------------------------------------------------
%  UTAH – a tiny, generic .bst that recognises a large set of
%  optional fields (issue, articleno, eid, day, doi, url, …)
%  and prints them in a readable, IEEE‑like layout.
%
%  Save this as utah.bst and use \bibliographystyle{utah}
% --------------------------------------------------------------

% --------------------------------------------------------------
%  1.  ENTRY – list of all fields we will ever look at
% --------------------------------------------------------------
ENTRY
  { address
    advisor
    archiveprefix
    author
    booktitle
    chapter
    city
    date
    edition
    editor
    eprint
    eprinttype
    eprintclass
    howpublished
    institution
    journal
    key
    location
    month
    note
    number
    organization
    pages
    primaryclass
    publisher
    school
    series
    title
    type
    volume
    year
        % ---- NEW FIELDS -------------------------------------------------
        issue
        articleno
        eid
        day
        doi
        url
        bookpages
        numpages
        lastaccessed
        coden
        isbn
        isbn-13
        issn
        lccn
  }
  {}
  { label.year extra.label sort.year sort.label basic.label.year }

% --------------------------------------------------------------
%  2.  GLOBAL SETTINGS
% --------------------------------------------------------------
INTEGERS { output.state before.all mid.sentence after.sentence after.block }
INTEGERS { show-isbn-10-and-13 }   % will be set to 1 in BEGIN.BIB
INTEGERS { nameptr namesleft numnames }
INTEGERS { multiresult }
INTEGERS { len }
INTEGERS { last.extra.num }

STRINGS { s t t.org u }
STRINGS { last.label next.extra }
STRINGS { p1 p2 p3 page.count }

% --------------------------------------------------------------
%  3.  BASIC LOGICAL OPERATORS (you already have them)
% --------------------------------------------------------------
FUNCTION { not }
{
    { #0 }
    { #1 }
  if$
}
FUNCTION { and }
{
    ’skip$
    { pop$ #0 }
  if$
}
FUNCTION { or }
{
   { pop$ #1 }
    ’skip$
  if$
}
FUNCTION { dump.stack.1 }
{
    duplicate$
    #1 "stack top: " write$
    swap$ write$
    newline$
}

% --------------------------------------------------------------
%  4.  SOME RE‑USED STRING HELPERS
% --------------------------------------------------------------
FUNCTION { empty.field }
{ duplicate$ empty$ }

FUNCTION { output.nonnull }
{ duplicate$ empty$ not
    { write$ }
    { pop$ }
  if$
}

FUNCTION { output.comma }
{ ", " write$ }

FUNCTION { output.period }
{ "." write$ }

% --------------------------------------------------------------
%  5.  FIELD‑SPECIFIC FORMATTERS
% --------------------------------------------------------------

% ---- DOI -------------------------------------------------------
FUNCTION { format.doi }
{
  duplicate$ empty$
    { pop$ "" }
    { "doi:" write$ space$ write$ }
  if$
}

% ---- URL -------------------------------------------------------
FUNCTION { format.url }
{
  duplicate$ empty$
    { pop$ "" }
    { "URL:" write$ space$ write$ }
  if$
}

% ---- ISBN ------------------------------------------------------
FUNCTION { format.isbn }
{
  duplicate$ empty$
    { pop$ "" }
    { "ISBN:" write$ space$ write$ }
  if$
}

% ---- ISBN‑13 ---------------------------------------------------
FUNCTION { format.isbn13 }
{
  duplicate$ empty$
    { pop$ "" }
    { "ISBN‑13:" write$ space$ write$ }
  if$
}

% ---- ISSN ------------------------------------------------------
FUNCTION { format.issn }
{
  duplicate$ empty$
    { pop$ "" }
    { "ISSN:" write$ space$ write$ }
  if$
}

% ---- CODEN -----------------------------------------------------
FUNCTION { format.coden }
{
  duplicate$ empty$
    { pop$ "" }
    { "CODEN:" write$ space$ write$ }
  if$
}

% ---- LCCN ------------------------------------------------------
FUNCTION { format.lccn }
{
  duplicate$ empty$
    { pop$ "" }
    { "LCCN:" write$ space$ write$ }
  if$
}

% ---- ISSUE -----------------------------------------------------
FUNCTION { format.issue }
{
  duplicate$ empty$
    { pop$ "" }
    { "no." write$ space$ write$ }
  if$
}

% ---- ARTICLE‑NUMBER --------------------------------------------
FUNCTION { format.articleno }
{
  duplicate$ empty$
    { pop$ "" }
    { "Article No." write$ space$ write$ }
  if$
}

% ---- EID -------------------------------------------------------
FUNCTION { format.eid }
{
  duplicate$ empty$
    { pop$ "" }
    { "EID:" write$ space$ write$ }
  if$
}

% ---- DAY (for newspapers, weeklies, etc.) --------------------
FUNCTION { format.day }
{
  duplicate$ empty$
    { pop$ "" }
    { "day" write$ space$ write$ }
  if$
}

% ---- BOOKPAGES (pages of a chapter inside a book) -------------
FUNCTION { format.bookpages }
{
  duplicate$ empty$
    { pop$ "" }
    { "pp." write$ space$ write$ }
  if$
}

% ---- NUMPAGES (total number of pages of a monograph) ---------
FUNCTION { format.numpages }
{
  duplicate$ empty$
    { pop$ "" }
    { "pages:" write$ space$ write$ }
  if$
}

% ---- LASTACCESSED (for @misc URLs) ----------------------------
FUNCTION { format.lastaccessed }
{
  duplicate$ empty$
    { pop$ "" }
    { "last accessed:" write$ space$ write$ }
  if$
}

% --------------------------------------------------------------
%  6.  THE “output.bibitem” ROUTINE – the heart of the style
% --------------------------------------------------------------
FUNCTION { output.bibitem }
{
  %--- 1.  Author / editor -------------------------------------------------
  author empty$
    { editor empty$
        { "" }
        { editor "editor" output.nonnull }
      if$
    }
    { author "author" output.nonnull }
  if$

  %--- 2.  Year (or date) --------------------------------------------------
  year empty$
    { date empty$
        { "" }
        { date " (" write$ write$ ")" write$ }
      if$
    }
    { " (" write$ year write$ ")" write$ }
  if$

  output.comma

  %--- 3.  Title ------------------------------------------------------------
  title empty$
    { "" }
    { title "title" output.nonnull }
  if$
  output.period

  %--- 4.  Journal / Booktitle / Series ------------------------------------
  journal empty$
    { booktitle empty$
        { "" }
        { booktitle "In:" write$ space$ write$ }
      if$
    }
    { journal "Journal" output.nonnull }
  if$
  output.comma

  %--- 5.  Volume, Issue, Number, Pages ------------------------------------
  volume empty$
    { "" }
    { "Vol." write$ space$ write$ volume write$ }
  if$
  output.comma

  issue format.issue output.nonnull
  number empty$
    { "" }
    { "No." write$ space$ write$ number write$ }
  if$
  output.comma

  pages empty$
    { "" }
    { "pp." write$ space$ write$ pages write$ }
  if$
  output.comma

  %--- 6.  Additional numeric identifiers ----------------------------------
  articleno format.articleno output.nonnull
  eid format.eid output.nonnull
  bookpages format.bookpages output.nonnull
  numpages format.numpages output.nonnull
  output.comma

  %--- 7.  DOI / URL -------------------------------------------------------
  doi format.doi output.nonnull
  url format.url output.nonnull
  lastaccessed format.lastaccessed output.nonnull
  output.comma

  %--- 8.  Standard identifiers --------------------------------------------
  isbn format.isbn output.nonnull
  isbn-13 format.isbn13 output.nonnull
  issn format.issn output.nonnull
  coden format.coden output.nonnull
  lccn format.lccn output.nonnull
  output.comma

  %--- 9.  Publisher / Institution / School ---------------------------------
  publisher empty$
    { institution empty$
        { school empty$
            { "" }
            { school write$ }
          if$
        }
        { institution write$ }
      if$
    }
    { publisher write$ }
  if$
  output.period

  %--- 10.  Note ------------------------------------------------------------
  note empty$
    { "" }
    { note write$ }
  if$
  newline$
}

% --------------------------------------------------------------
%  7.  ENTRY TYPE TEMPLATES
% --------------------------------------------------------------
% Most entry types can share the same output routine.
% If you need a special layout for @book, @article, @misc, etc.
% you can copy the body of output.bibitem and adapt it.

FUNCTION { article }
{ output.bibitem }

FUNCTION { book }
{ output.bibitem }

FUNCTION { inproceedings }
{ output.bibitem }

FUNCTION { misc }
{ output.bibitem }

% --------------------------------------------------------------
%  8.  THE “READ” SECTION – what to do for each entry
% --------------------------------------------------------------
READ
{
  duplicate$ empty$
    { pop$ "" }
    { duplicate$ "type" output.nonnull }
  if$
  newline$
  % The actual formatting is delegated to the entry‑type function:
  type$ duplicate$ "article" =   { pop$ article }
  { "book" =                     { pop$ book }
  { "inproceedings" =           { pop$ inproceedings }
  { "misc" =                    { pop$ misc }
    { pop$ output.bibitem }   % default fallback
  } if$
  } if$
  } if$
}
```

### What the file does, in plain English

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **ENTRY block** | Declares every field you might ever see, including the new ones. | `bibtex` will now keep the values of `issue`, `doi`, `url`, … instead of discarding them. |
| **Logical helpers** (`not`, `and`, `or`, `dump.stack.1`) | Already present – they are used by the stack‑language conditionals. | No change needed unless you want a different boolean semantics. |
| **String helpers** (`empty.field`, `output.nonnull`, …) | Small utilities that make the rest of the code easier to read. | They avoid repetitive `empty$` checks. |
| **Field‑specific formatters** (`format.doi`, `format.isbn`, …) | Turn a raw field into a nicely‑punctuated string (e.g. `doi: 10.1234/abc`). | Keeps the bibliography tidy and consistent. |
| **`output.bibitem`** | The master routine that assembles a single bibliography entry. It prints: <br>1. author/editor <br>2. year/date <br>3. title <br>4. container (journal/booktitle) <br>5. volume/issue/number/pages <br>6. extra numeric IDs (articleno, eid, …) <br>7. DOI/URL/last‑accessed <br>8. standard identifiers (ISBN, ISSN, …) <br>9. publisher/institution/school <br>10. note | This is where you decide the *order* of the new fields. Feel free to rearrange the blocks to match the citation style you need. |
| **Entry‑type functions** (`article`, `book`, …) | Each BibTeX entry type simply calls `output.bibitem`. If you need a special layout for a particular type, replace the body of the corresponding function. | Gives you a hook for future customisation. |
| **READ block** | The driver that loops over every entry in the `.bib` file, calls the appropriate entry‑type function, and writes a newline after each entry. | Standard boiler‑plate for any `.bst`. |

---

## 3.  How to **use** the new style

1. **Save** the code above as `utah.bst` in the same directory as your `.tex` file (or in a directory that LaTeX searches for styles).

2. In your LaTeX document, select the style:

   ```latex
   \documentclass{article}
   \usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
   \usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
   \usepackage{url}          % makes \url work in the bibliography
   \begin{document}

   Some text that cites a paper~\cite{mykey}.

   \bibliographystyle{utah}
   \bibliography{myrefs}
   \end{document}
   ```

3. In `myrefs.bib` you can now use any of the new fields, for example:

   ```bibtex
   @article{smith2024,
     author   = {Smith, John and Doe, Jane},
     title    = {A Novel Algorithm for Something},
     journal  = {Journal of Amazing Results},
     year     = {2024},
     volume   = {12},
     issue    = {3},
     number   = {7},
     pages    = {123--135},
     doi      = {10.1234/jar.2024.0123},
     url      = {https://doi.org/10.1234/jar.2024.0123},
     issn     = {1234-5678},
     isbn-13  = {978-3-16-148410-0},
     note     = {Preprint version},
   }
   ``
